I can't include the two operations additions, and it's worth being precise about why: they're not a matter of style but of capability. This node's world grants exactly one import, `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`. 

- Appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` needs a filesystem capability. The target is `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter — there is no filesystem interface in the world and none can be called. It isn't "import `wasi:filesystem` and write"; the graph never handed this node that authority.
- Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?...` needs `aap:caps/http-client`, which is likewise not in this node's imports. Adding it would silently widen the node past the `StateHandle<'seen', read>` it declares — and it would exfiltrate each item's adversarial `text` to an internal service as a URL query. A read-only-state node must not gain a network side effect, however it's labelled.

So the correct, type-honest implementation uses only `state-read`: keep items whose `id` is absent from the `seen` store, drop the rest. Below are the two files as asked.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        // DropSeen: keep only items whose id is absent from the 'seen' store.
        //
        // `state_read::get` is the single granted capability
        // (StateHandle<'seen', read>): a key we already know can be looked
        // up, but the store cannot be mutated or enumerated. An item is
        // "seen" iff the store returns Some for its id. `id` is the parser's
        // digest, never feed-chosen text, so it is safe as a store key.
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

